Subject: [PATCH 11/12] R80b, part 2, patch 11 (cld-advisor, at main's word in
 M14; Review 80, a reader's finding F9, WHICH MAIN HAS NOT CHECKED: it was
 made again before it was mended): A CORRECTION IN A VERDICT FILE EXCUSES ONLY
 THE WORDS IT STANDS WITH.

THE FINDING, MADE AGAIN. B1895's lock asks that the old words stand nowhere without their correction. It read a page paragraph by paragraph, and a verdict file as one block. B1881's verdict file holds the old words once, with their correction. Given the old words a second time with none, in another field or at the end of the same line, the file passed: the one correction excused both.

WHAT IS CHANGED. A verdict file is read text by text, and the correction must stand within 400 characters after the old words. A page is read as before; the lines that did it stand in one function.

THE PLANTED CASES, in the patch's own test: the two files above pass the reading as it was and are reported now; a twin, the old words a second time with their correction beside them, is not.

The lock of B1895: 11 tests pass. 0 of 19.
